course: dictionary
lesson: 13286
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «My sister will graduate from university next summer» means she will finish her course and get her degree. Here graduate is a verb, an action word.
explain: Look at the words around it. «He graduated to the big bike after learning on the small one» means he moved up to something harder or bigger. The clue is "after learning on the small one": he moved on to a new step, he did not finish a course.
explain: Some words look the same but mean something different. «The measuring jug is graduated, with a line every 50 millilitres» means the jug is marked with lines for measuring. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then check by trying it in place of the word.
fact: Graduate can be a verb (to graduate) or a noun (a graduate).
fact: «Graduate» means to finish a course at university, college or, in the US, school, and get a degree or diploma.
fact: «Graduate» can also mean to move up to something harder or bigger, as in «graduate to a big bike».
fact: «A graduate» is a person who has finished university.
fact: «Graduated» can describe a jug or cylinder marked with lines for measuring.
exercise: «My cousin will graduate from university this summer.» What does graduate mean here? | Stop eating dinner | Fall over while running | Move house to a new town | *Finish a course and get a degree
exercise: «She graduated to the top reading group after the summer.» What does graduated mean here? | Read a book backwards | Left the school for good | *Moved up to something harder | Painted a picture
exercise: «The measuring jug is graduated, with a line every 50 millilitres.» What does graduated mean? | Covered in paint | *Marked with lines for measuring | Made of glass only | Finished and put away
test: «Mia is a graduate of the college.» What is a graduate? | A teacher's helper | *A person who has finished university | A student who is still in class | A kind of sports team
test: «The rider graduated from the small bike to a bigger one.» What does graduated mean? | Crashed into a wall | *Moved up to something bigger | Sold the bike | Rode it in the rain
exam: «After four years of study, Tom will graduate, and he hopes to graduate to a job as an engineer.» What does graduate mean in the second use? | Take a long holiday | Finish a course | *Move up to something harder or bigger | Measure with lines
